--TEST--
Core\Task::afterResponse refuses a registration made from inside deferred work — the drain seals the queue as it takes it — where a PHP shutdown function may register another that then runs, so the queue that extends itself has no spelling here
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`register_shutdown_function` appends to a list PHP is already walking, so a shutdown function may register another one and PHP runs it in the same drain; the queue here is *taken* by the drain and sealed as it goes, so the same call is a catchable throw instead — `crates/nvs-runtime/src/deferred.rs`'s *Each registration is one task, and the queue is a leaf* section is the home of why, and the sentence that decides it is that a queue which can extend itself is a request tree that never leaves flight, while ADR 0072 § 6's whole affordability argument is that the tree outlives the connection only *a little*. The refusal is one rule rather than two: deferred work runs on a sealed context exactly as a `Core\Task` child and an isolate do, which is why the message names all three. Nothing about the ordering half diverges — `task-after-response-defers-in-registration-order-like-register_shutdown_function` measures that against the same PHP member and they agree — and a program that wants the second piece of work still gets it by registering both from the request, which is the last two lines below.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The registration that is refused, and the one that is not: the difference is
// only *where the call is made from*, so both are written here rather than
// argued about. `nvs_runtime::deferred` owns the rule; this case is what says
// the refusal is catchable and reaches the program as a message rather than
// ending the request.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "deferred ran\n";
    try {
        Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
            echo "this never runs\n";
        });
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        echo "refused: ", $e->message, "\n";
    }
});
echo "response written\n";

// Both pieces of work, registered from the request itself, which is the
// spelling a PHP program's nested `register_shutdown_function` becomes. The
// order is the registration order the neighbouring case pins.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "first\n";
});
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "second\n";
});
--EXPECT--
response written
deferred ran
refused: Core\Task::afterResponse: only the request's own task may defer work, and this is a child task — a `Core\Task` child, an isolate, or deferred work itself; hand it back to the request that started you
first
second
